sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch16-09> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Because the jointed legs cannot resist the horizontal thrust of an arch and there is no tie between its ends, the backbone of a standing quadruped is not an arch, tied arch or bowstring girder but is mechanically comparable to the main girder of a double-armed cantilever bridge.";
    dc:type "mathematical-law";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "The quadruped backbone is a double-armed cantilever girder, not an arch";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "D’Arcy Wentworth Thompson, On Growth and Form (1st ed., Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1917), Chapter XVI, “On Form and Mechanical Efficiency”; topic “Quadruped skeleton as a bridge”. Scope: vertebral column and associated ligaments of standing quadrupeds"
}
